---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'not-medical-advice', label: 'Not medical advice' },
  { id: 'the-numbers', label: 'The numbers' },
  { id: 'who-should-be-careful', label: 'Who should be careful' },
  { id: 'supplements', label: 'Supplements' },
  { id: 'in-an-emergency', label: 'In an emergency' },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Disclaimer"
  description="What Eidolon is and isn't, in plain language — not medical advice, estimates not lab results."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="not-medical-advice">Not medical advice</h2>
  <p>
    Eidolon gives general educational information about food and training. It is not medical advice,
    and it doesn't replace a consultation with a doctor or a registered dietitian. If you have a
    medical condition, take regular medicines, or are pregnant or breastfeeding, talk to your doctor
    before changing how you eat or train. Nothing on this site diagnoses or treats anything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-numbers">The numbers are estimates</h2>
  <p>
    Your calorie, protein and other targets come from standard formulas (Mifflin-St Jeor for
    calories, grams of protein per kg of body weight, and similar). They're a reasonable starting
    point, not a lab measurement of your body. Results vary from person to person, and a plan that
    fits one person's week won't fit everyone's.
  </p>
  <p>
    The food values behind each dish — calories, protein, carbs, fat — are careful estimates built
    from standard food composition references. They have <strong>not</strong> yet been reviewed by a
    dietitian. I'm working through that review dish by dish; until a dish is marked reviewed, treat
    its numbers as a close estimate, not a certified figure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-should-be-careful">Who should be careful</h2>
  <p>
    Plans here are built for adults. If you tell the questionnaire you're under 18, you get general
    food guidance with no calorie or weight-loss numbers — growing bodies need an adult who knows
    them, not an app, making that call. The same general-guidance mode applies if you're pregnant or
    breastfeeding, or if a safety check flags something that needs a doctor's judgement rather than
    a formula's.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're on medicines for diabetes, a heart condition, or you're 65 or older, any calorie
    reduction the plan suggests is kept small on purpose, and the plan asks you to check with your
    doctor first. These rules exist because a formula can't see your test results or your medicine
    doses — your doctor can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="supplements">Supplements</h2>
  <p>
    Every plan gets its protein and nutrients from food first. Where supplements come up — in an
    article or a product pick — I don't claim any supplement cures or treats a condition. Anything I
    recommend is a food-first suggestion, not a substitute for a balanced diet or for medical
    treatment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-an-emergency">In an emergency</h2>
  <p>
    This site cannot help in a medical emergency. If you or someone around you needs urgent medical
    care, contact a doctor or your local emergency services right away — don't wait on anything
    you've read here.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
